      "text": "Great news — I've gathered enough information to give you a solid background. Here's what you need to know about the publication history and controversy surrounding *Madame Bovary*:\n\n---\n\n## 📖 *Madame Bovary* — Publication History & Controversy\n\n### First Publication (1856–1857)\n- ***Madame Bovary* was first published serially in *La Revue de Paris*** from **October to December 1856**, before appearing as a complete novel in book form in **1857**.\n- The novel was authored by **Gustave Flaubert** (1821–1880) and is set in provincial Normandy, France.\n\n### The Obscenity Trial (1857)\nThe serialization immediately sparked **one of the most famous literary controversies of the 19th century**:\n- The French government **prosecuted Flaubert** (along with the magazine's editors) on charges of **immorality and offenses against public morals and religion**. The novel was seen as glamorizing adultery and portraying bourgeois and religious life in an unflattering, scandalous light.\n- The trial took place in **January 1857** and drew enormous public attention.\n- **Flaubert was ultimately acquitted**, but the trial had an unintended effect: it made the novel enormously famous. When the book edition was published later in 1857 by **Michel Lévy Frères**, it was an immediate sensation.\n\n### Why Was It So Controversial?\n- The novel follows **Emma Bovary**, a doctor's wife in rural France, who pursues **extramarital affairs** and accumulates debt in a desperate search for the passionate, romantic life she had imagined from reading novels.\n- Critics found its frank depiction of **female desire, adultery, and bourgeois hypocrisy** deeply transgressive.\n- Flaubert's narrative technique — especially his use of **free indirect discourse** (blending Emma's inner thoughts seamlessly with the narrator's voice) — made it hard to morally condemn Emma outright, which troubled authorities who wanted a clear moral lesson.\n\n### Legacy\n- The trial, paradoxically, cemented the novel's place in literary history. It is now considered **one of the greatest novels ever written** and a landmark of **literary realism**.\n- Key themes still discussed in courses like yours include: **romanticism vs. realism, bourgeois life, desire and disillusionment, gender and constraint**, and Flaubert's revolutionary narrative style.\n\n---\n\nThis should give you a strong foundation heading into your course! Would you like me to pull an excerpt from the novel itself, or find some critical essays to help with your studies?",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
