    "finalPromptText": "That's really helpful, thanks! Can you give me a general summary of the novel's plot and main characters? I want to have a good overview before I start reading so I can follow the class discussions more easily.",
    "finalAssistantVisibleText": "I have the opening of the novel from the text retrieval, and combined with my established knowledge of this well-documented classic, I can give you a thorough and accurate overview. Here it is:\n\n---\n\n## 📚 *Madame Bovary* — Plot Summary & Main Characters\n\n> ⚠️ **Note: Full plot summary below — includes spoilers!** Since you mentioned wanting to be prepared for class discussions, I've included the complete arc. Skip to \"Main Characters\" if you'd prefer to read the plot yourself first.\n\n---\n\n### Structure\nThe novel is divided into **three parts**, tracking the life of Emma Bovary from her marriage through her tragic downfall.\n\n---\n\n### 🗂️ Part One — Marriage and Disappointment\nThe novel opens with **Charles Bovary** as a clumsy, unremarkable schoolboy — a telling introduction to a man who will never be extraordinary. Charles becomes a country doctor and, after the death of his first wife, meets and marries **Emma Rouault**, the educated, romantically-minded daughter of a local farmer.\n\nEmma had been raised in a convent, where she devoured Romantic novels and developed extravagant fantasies about love, passion, and refined society. She expects marriage to deliver all of this. Instead, she finds Charles to be dull, unambitious, and incapable of the grand passion she craves. Their life in the village of **Tostes** feels suffocatingly mediocre to her.\n\nA pivotal moment comes when they are invited to a grand ball at the estate of the Marquis d'Andervilliers — Emma gets a fleeting taste of aristocratic elegance, which only deepens her discontent. She sinks into depression and illness, and Charles, worried about her health, decides to relocate the family to a new town.\n\n---\n\n### 🗂️ Part Two — Affairs and Illusions\nThe family moves to **Yonville-l'Abbaye**, a slightly larger but equally provincial town, where Emma gives birth to a daughter, **Berthe** — a child she largely ignores, having hoped for a son.\n\nHere Emma meets two key figures:\n- **Léon Dupuis**, a young law clerk who shares her love of literature and romantic ideals. They are clearly drawn to each other, but neither acts on it — Léon eventually leaves for Paris to continue his studies.\n- **Rodolphe Boulanger**, a wealthy, cynical local landowner who seduces Emma with practiced ease. He knows exactly what she wants to hear, and they begin a passionate affair.\n\nEmma throws herself into the relationship with Rodolphe, fantasizing that they will run away together and leave her dreary life behind. But when the moment comes, Rodolphe — who never truly loved her — coldly writes her a letter calling off the elopement and disappears. Emma is devastated and suffers a serious mental and physical breakdown.\n\nDuring her recovery, she turns temporarily to religion and becomes closer to Charles, who, desperate to make her happy, arranges for her to attend the opera in **Rouen** — where she encounters **Léon** again.\n\n---\n\n### 🗂️ Part Three — Debt, Despair, and Destruction\nEmma and Léon begin an affair. She makes regular trips to Rouen under false pretexts, and the relationship, while passionate at first, gradually becomes routine and disillusioning — just like everything else in Emma's life.\n\nMeanwhile, Emma has been recklessly spending money on clothes, furnishings, and gifts, egged on by **Lheureux**, a cunning, manipulative merchant who has been extending her credit for years. The debts spiral catastrophically out of control. When the bills finally come due, Emma desperately tries to raise money — she begs Léon, attempts to seduce Rodolphe for funds, and approaches various townspeople — all in vain.\n\nFacing complete financial ruin and the seizure of their household, Emma walks to the pharmacy of **Homais** and, in a moment of desperation, swallows a fistful of arsenic. Her death is prolonged and agonizing. Charles, grief-stricken, soon discovers the truth about both her affairs and their debts. He dies shortly afterward, heartbroken and ruined, leaving little **Berthe** an orphan, sent to work in a cotton mill.\n\nThe novel closes with a darkly ironic coda: **Homais**, the smug, self-satisfied pharmacist, is awarded the Legion of Honor — the mediocre world Emma despised goes on, undisturbed.\n\n---\n\n## 👥 Main Characters\n\n| Character | Role | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Emma Bovary** | Protagonist | The central figure — passionate, imaginative, and deeply dissatisfied. Her \"Bovarism\" (a term coined from this novel) refers to the tendency to romanticize one's life and confuse fantasy with reality. Sympathetic yet deeply flawed. |\n| **Charles Bovary** | Emma's husband | A kind but hopelessly dull country doctor. He adores Emma completely but is utterly blind to her inner life. His mediocrity is the cage Emma cannot escape. |\n| **Rodolphe Boulanger** | First lover | A wealthy landowner and experienced seducer. He pursues Emma knowingly and abandons her just as deliberately. Represents the cold reality behind romantic fantasy. |\n| **Léon Dupuis** | Second lover | A young, sensitive law clerk. He and Emma share genuine intellectual affinity, but their affair ultimately disappoints her, as everything does. |\n| **Monsieur Lheureux** | Merchant/moneylender | One of the novel's true villains. He patiently entangles Emma in debt, knowing she will eventually be ruined. A symbol of the predatory nature of capitalism and consumerism. |\n| **Monsieur Homais** | Pharmacist | A pompous, self-important village intellectual who represents the smug complacency of provincial bourgeois life. His reward at the novel's end is bitterly ironic. |\n| **Berthe Bovary** | Emma & Charles's daughter | A largely neglected child whose fate — ending up an orphaned factory worker — underscores the human cost of Emma's choices. |\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Themes to Watch For in Class\n- **\"Bovarism\"** — the danger of confusing romantic fantasy with reality\n- **The failure of Romanticism** — Flaubert is writing a critique of the very type of novels Emma loves\n- **Gender and constraint** — Emma is trapped by 19th-century expectations of women\n- **Consumerism and desire** — Emma's spending is an extension of her emotional hunger\n- **The banality of bourgeois life** — Flaubert's deeply ironic portrait of provincial France\n\n---\n\nThis should have you very well-prepared for class discussions! Would you like me to pull any specific passages from the text, or find some critical essays or study guides to go deeper?",
