rward, heartbroken and ruined, leaving little **Berthe** an orphan, sent to work in a cotton mill.\n\nThe novel closes with a darkly ironic coda: **Homais**, the smug, self-satisfied pharmacist, is awarded the Legion of Honor — the mediocre world Emma despised goes on, undisturbed.\n\n---\n\n## 👥 Main Characters\n\n| Character | Role | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Emma Bovary** | Protagonist | The central figure — passionate, imaginative, and deeply dissatisfied. Her \"Bovarism\" (a term coined from this novel) refers to the tendency to romanticize one's life and confuse fantasy with reality. Sympathetic yet deeply flawed. |\n| **Charles Bovary** | Emma's husband | A kind but hopelessly dull country doctor. He adores Emma completely but is utterly blind to her inner life. His mediocrity is the cage Emma cannot escape. |\n| **Rodolphe Boulanger** | First lover | A wealthy landowner and experienced seducer. He pursues Emma knowingly and abandons her just as deliberately. Represents the cold reality behind romantic fantasy. |\n| **Léon Dupuis** | Second lover | A young, sensitive law clerk. He and Emma share genuine intellectual affinity, but their affair ultimately disappoints her, as everything does. |\n| **Monsieur Lheureux** | Merchant/moneylender | One of the novel's true villains. He patiently entangles Emma in debt, knowing she will eventually be ruined. A symbol of the predatory nature of capitalism and consumerism. |\n| **Monsieur Homais** | Pharmacist | A pompous, self-important village intellectual who represents the smug complacency of provincial bourgeois life. His reward at the novel's end is bitterly ironic. |\n| **Berthe Bovary** | Emma & Charles's daughter | A largely neglected child whose fate — ending up an orphaned factory worker — underscores the human cost of Emma's choices. |\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Key Themes to Watch For in Class\n- **\"Bovarism\"** — the danger of confusing romantic fantasy with reality\n- **The failure of Romanticism** — Flaubert